<section class="block">
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p><strong>HTI-5 is final, and narrower than proposed.</strong> The US health IT certification rule was finalised in late October. Legal analyses report it removes 14 of 60 certification criteria rather than the 34 proposed, keeps the privacy and security criteria the proposal would have dropped, defers proposed information blocking changes, and removes the AI "model card" requirements for predictive decision support interventions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. We covered the proposal in <a href="/insights/hti-1-to-hti-5-health-it-ai-rules/">From HTI-1 to HTI-5</a>; the final rule confirms the direction on AI transparency while retreating on much else.</p>
<p><strong>A Senate draft rethinks oversight and payment.</strong> On 29 October, Senate HELP Committee chair Bill Cassidy released a discussion draft of the PATIENT AI Act. It would clarify whether an AI tool falls under FDA or ONC oversight, let FDA accept premarket software changes, have HHS certify third parties to review lower-risk software, and create a payment pathway for AI-enabled care; comments are due by 1 December<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. It is a draft, not law.</p>
<p><strong>WHO Europe sets a direction.</strong> The same day, governments across Europe and Central Asia adopted a roadmap for AI in health care, emphasising foundations such as data quality, interoperability, secure access and cybersecurity<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What it means for builders</h2>
<p>In the US, certification will ask less of AI vendors about transparency. That does not mean buyers will. Health systems still have to decide whether a model is safe and useful for their patients, and with less standard documentation, they will ask vendors directly and test more themselves. If the PATIENT AI Act's ideas progress, payment for AI-enabled care would come with expectations of evidence. In Europe, the roadmap points the same way through data quality and interoperability. Different routes, one destination: show the model works, here, on these patients.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Regulation</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>HTI-5<em>fewer federal AI disclosure rules</em></span><span>PATIENT AI Act draft<em>oversight and payment</em></span><span>WHO Europe roadmap<em>data and trust foundations</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Buyer expectations</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Model documentation<em>now asked for directly</em></span><span class="hl">Local validation<em>on the buyer's population</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>What to build</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Model facts<em>versioned with releases</em></span><span>Validation pipeline<em>runs per site</em></span><span>Monitoring<em>drift and outcomes</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Data foundations</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>FHIR APIs<em>still central to certification</em></span><span>Common data model<em>for portable validation</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>As formal disclosure rules shrink, buyers' evidence requirements grow. Build for the buyer.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Build the evidence anyway</h2>
<p><strong>Keep your model facts.</strong> Maintain the information the model card requirement covered, such as intended use, training data, known limitations and performance, versioned with every release. It costs little if built into the release process, and buyers will keep asking for it.</p>
<p><strong>Make validation portable.</strong> A model that performed well where it was built may not perform well elsewhere. The OHDSI community frames this as evidence attributes: results should be repeatable, reproducible, replicable, generalisable to different data and robust to different analyses<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. Mapping site data to a common model such as OMOP lets you run the same validation at each customer; see <a href="/insights/clinical-prediction-models-omop/">clinical prediction models on OMOP</a>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/health-ai-rules-hti-5-patient-ai-act/reliable-evidence.webp" alt="Table of desired evidence attributes: repeatable, reproducible, replicable, generalisable, robust and calibrated, showing which of question, researcher, data and analysis stay identical or change and whether results should be identical, similar or statistically consistent" width="1400" height="486" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Generalisable means similar results on different data: exactly what a health system needs to know before deploying your model. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://ohdsi.github.io/TheBookOfOhdsi/EvidenceQuality.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Book of OHDSI, The OHDSI Community</a> · CC0 1.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Plan for software change.</strong> If FDA gains clearer authority to accept planned software changes, teams with a disciplined change process will move faster. See <a href="/insights/fda-pccp-ai-medical-devices/">predetermined change control plans</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Keep FHIR at the centre.</strong> The FHIR API criteria stay, and the direction of travel in both the US and Europe is towards standard, secure data access; see <a href="/insights/tefca-2026-what-health-tech-should-build/">TEFCA in 2026</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Regulation sets the minimum. A health system's procurement team sets the bar. Build your evidence for the bar.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Next 90 days</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Weeks 1 to 4</small><b>Document</b><ul><li>Model facts per product</li><li>Versioned with releases</li><li>Plain-language summary</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 5 to 8</small><b>Validate</b><ul><li>Site validation pipeline</li><li>Common data model mapping</li><li>Subgroup performance</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 9 to 12</small><b>Engage</b><ul><li>Comment on the draft by 1 December</li><li>Brief key customers</li><li>Monitoring in production</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Document, validate, then use the comment window to shape what comes next.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>The US is stepping back from prescribing AI transparency through certification while exploring how to pay for AI that works, and Europe is investing in the data foundations that make evidence possible. For health tech teams, that adds up to one strategy: own your evidence. Teams that can show, per customer, what their model is, how it performs on that customer's patients and how it is monitored will sell faster under any of these regimes. See <a href="/healthcare-data-architecture/">healthcare data architecture</a>, the <a href="/fractional-cto-united-states/">US page</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe page</a>.</p>
</section>

